<question type="multichoice">
  <name><text>NEET_UG_PHYSICS_OSCILLATIONS_MCQ_Q14_16 - Physical quantities from displacement time graph</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The displacement time graph of a particle executing S.H.M. is shown in Fig. 14.5. Which of the following statement is/are true?</p>
<p><img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/img-4.jpeg" alt="Fig. 14.5" /></p>]]></text>
  
      <file name="img-4.jpeg" encoding="base64"></file>
    </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Curve Analysis</strong><br />The graph represents simple harmonic motion with displacement starting at positive maximum at \(t = 0\):<br />\[x(t) = A \cos(\omega t) = A \cos\left(\frac{2\pi t}{T}\right)\]<br />Velocity is given by \(v(t) = -\omega A \sin\left(\frac{2\pi t}{T}\right)\).<br />Acceleration is given by \(a(t) = -\omega^2 x(t) = -\omega^2 A \cos\left(\frac{2\pi t}{T}\right)\).<br />Restoring force is \(F(t) = -k x(t) = -m\omega^2 x(t)\).</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Evaluation at Specific Time Instants</strong><br />At \(t = \frac{T}{4}\):<br />\[x = A \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) = 0\]<br />The particle is passing through the mean position, where the magnitude of velocity is maximum: \(|v| = \omega A = v_{\max}\). Hence, the velocity is maximum at \(t = \frac{T}{4}\).<br />At \(t = \frac{T}{2} = \frac{2T}{4}\):<br />\[x = A \cos(\pi) = -A\]<br />The particle is at the negative extreme position. Its velocity is zero, so kinetic energy is zero (\(\text{K.E.} = 0\)), while potential energy is maximum (\(\text{P.E.} = \frac{1}{2}kA^2\)). Thus, potential energy is not equal to kinetic energy at \(t = \frac{T}{2}\).<br />At \(t = \frac{3T}{4}\):<br />\[x = A \cos\left(\frac{3\pi}{2}\right) = 0\]<br />The displacement is zero, so the restoring force \(F = -kx = 0\). Hence, the force is zero at \(t = \frac{3T}{4}\).<br />At \(t = \frac{4T}{4} = T\):<br />\[x = A \cos(2\pi) = A\]<br />The particle is at the positive extreme position, where displacement has maximum magnitude, so the magnitude of acceleration \(|a| = \omega^2 A\) is maximum. Hence, the acceleration is maximum at \(t = \frac{4T}{4}\).</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Check and Final Conclusion</strong><br />The true statements are that the force is zero at \(t = \frac{3T}{4}\), the acceleration is maximum at \(t = \frac{4T}{4}\), and the velocity is maximum at \(t = \frac{T}{4}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <single>false</single>
  <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
  <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
  <answer fraction="33.33333" format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The force is zero at \( t = \frac{3T}{4} \).</p>]]></text>
  </answer>
  <answer fraction="33.33333" format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The acceleration is maximum at \( t = \frac{4T}{4} \).</p>]]></text>
  </answer>
  <answer fraction="33.33333" format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The velocity is maximum at \( t = \frac{T}{4} \).</p>]]></text>
  </answer>
  <answer fraction="-50" format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The P.E. is equal to K.E. of oscillation at \( t = \frac{T}{2} \).</p>]]></text>
  </answer>
